  "prompt": "Feature: add a recipe asset from an image URL (drag-and-drop images from other sites).\n\nDragging an image out of another browser tab onto the recipe assets drop zone gives the page a URL, not a file, and the browser cannot download it on the site's behalf (CORS). The server has to fetch it. The frontend side (the DropZone component emitting drop-url, data: URLs converted to files, unusable blob: and javascript: URIs reported) is not graded.\n\nExpected:\n1. POST /api/recipes/{slug}/assets/url for authenticated users (anonymous gives 401, as the server would otherwise fetch any URL it is handed), body the URL import's model: {\"url\": \"<image url>\"}. It downloads the image server-side and stores the bytes unchanged as a recipe asset through the same path as an uploaded asset: the allowed-extension check (so an image/svg+xml download is refused with 400 and nothing is written), the slugified file name with the extension, de-duplication of an existing name, and the asset appended to the recipe. The asset's icon is mdi-file-image and its extension comes from the response's content type, not from the URL. It answers 200 with the RecipeAsset (name, icon, fileName), and GET /api/recipes/{slug} then lists the asset with that fileName.\n2. The download goes through RecipeDataService.fetch_image(image_url, max_bytes=None) -> tuple[bytes, str] | None (new), called with max_bytes=ASSET_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES, a constant of 5 * 1024 * 1024 in the recipe CRUD routes module (the tests import it from there). fetch_image calls safehttp.resilient_fetch(image_url, allow_flaresolverr=False, max_bytes=max_bytes); returns None when the fetch returns None or raises anything else (logged); re-raises safehttp.InvalidDomainError as the data service's own InvalidDomainError and lets safehttp.ResponseTooLargeError propagate; raises NotAnImageError unless the content type, lower-cased and stripped of parameters, starts with \"image/\" (\"text/html; name=image.png\", \"application/octet-stream\" and a missing header are not images); and returns the bytes with the subtype as the extension (\"IMAGE/JPEG; charset=binary\" gives \"jpeg\", \"image/png\" gives \"png\").\n3. The endpoint answers 400 for NotAnImageError (\"Url is not an image\"), InvalidDomainError (\"Url is not from an allowed domain\"), ResponseTooLargeError (\"Image is larger than 5MB\") and a None download (\"Image could not be downloaded\"), each as an ErrorResponse message, writing nothing.\n4. resilient_fetch gains max_bytes: int | None = None, passed down to each attempt. A declared Content-Length above it raises ResponseTooLargeError (message containing \"declared content-length\") before any of the body is read; a body that grows past it while streaming raises ResponseTooLargeError (\"response body exceeds\"), also when Content-Length is missing or understated; either way the fetch stops after that one attempt (no rotation). HEAD ignores the cap. Without max_bytes nothing is capped. ResponseTooLargeError is exported from the safehttp package.\n5. scrape_image (recipe images) now downloads through fetch_image with no cap (the minifier re-encodes to webp anyway) and writes the bytes with the content-type extension (\"image/png\" gives write_image(bytes, \"png\"), not the old URL-derived \"jpg\" guess). It still accepts a schema.org image dict, picks the largest of a list with largest_content_len, raises ValueError for unparseable input and returns None without writing when nothing downloads.\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests tests/integration_tests/user_recipe_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).\n",
  "title": "feat: support drag-and-drop images from web (#8493)",
